se 3, `reg.mystyle is not style_before`? That would verify re-rendering created a new object \u2014 depends on `__setattr__` always creating a new Style (it does: `Style(*rules, value=rendered)`). Safe to assert `reg.mystyle is not style_before`. Hmm, it's a valid postcondition proving setattr executed. But is it necessary? The value change already proves it. I'll include it anyway \u2014 it directly evidences line 156's effect. Actually, careful: could `setattr` with the same rendered value somehow keep the same object? No \u2014 `__setattr__` constructs `rendered_style = Style(*rules, value=rendered)` \u2014 always a new object. So `is not` holds. I'll include it.\n\nWait, actually one more subtle thing: in phase 3, `val = getattr(self, 'mystyle')` \u2192 style_before. `setattr(self, 'mystyle', style_before)` \u2192 new object with re-rendered value. So after: `reg.mystyle is not style_before` and value changed. Both assertions valid.\n\nFinal code:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Tests for sty.primitive.Register.set_renderfunc (lines 150-156).\"\"\"\n\nfrom sty.primitive import Register, Style\nfrom sty.rendertype import RenderType\n\n\nclass _DummyRenderType(RenderType):\n    \"\"\"A custom render type used as key for the renderfuncs mapping.\"\"\"\n\n\ndef _render_func_one(rule):\n    return \"<rendered-one>\"\n\n\ndef _render_func_two(rule):\n    return \"<rendered-two>\"\n\n\ndef test_set_renderfunc_registers_func_and_rerenders_style_attributes():\n    \"\"\"set_renderfunc stores the func and re-renders existing Style attrs.\"\"\"\n    reg = Register()\n\n    # Phase 1: register a renderfunc while no Style attributes exist yet\n    # (loop body's isinstance check is False for every attribute).\n    reg.set_renderfunc(_DummyRenderType, _render_func_one)\n    assert reg.renderfuncs == {_DummyRenderType: _render_func_one}\n\n    # Phase 2: attach a Style attribute; it is rendered with func one.\n    rule = _DummyRenderType()\n    reg.mystyle = Style(rule)\n    assert isinstance(reg.mystyle, Style)\n    assert str(reg.mystyle) == \"<rendered-one>\"\n    assert rule in reg.mystyle.rules\n\n    # A non-Style attribute that must survive the update loop untouched.\n    reg.plain = \"untouched\"\n    assert reg.plain == \"untouched\"\n\n    # Phase 3: replace the renderfunc. The already existing Style attribute\n    # must now be re-rendered with the new function (lines 153-156).\n    style_before = reg.mystyle\n    reg.set_renderfunc(_DummyRenderType, _render_func_two)\n\n    assert reg.renderfuncs == {_DummyRenderType: _render_func_two}\n    assert reg.mystyle is not style_before  # setattr re-created the Style.\n    assert isinstance(reg.mystyle, Style)\n    assert str(reg.mystyle) == \"<rendered-two>\"\n    assert rule in reg.mystyle.rules\n    assert reg.plain == \"untouched\"\n\n\ndef test_set_renderfunc_without_style_attributes():\n    \"\"\"set_renderfunc also works when the register holds no Style attrs.\"\"\"\n    reg = Register()\n    reg.set_renderfunc(_DummyRenderType, _render_func_one)\n\n    assert reg.renderfuncs == {_DummyRenderType: _render_func_one}\n    assert reg.is_muted is False\n```\n\nHmm, the docstring at top \u2014 fine. No pytest import needed. No fixtures. No cleanup needed beyond fresh instances (state is instance-local). \n\nWait \u2014 should I double check that `reg.is_muted is False` \u2014 `__init__` sets `self.is_muted = False`. Yes.\n\nOne concern: in test 1 phase 2, `reg.mystyle = Style(rule)` \u2014 `Style.__new__` sets `.rules` via `setattr(new_cls, 'rules', rules)` \u2014 note `new_cls` is the str instance. So `.rules` on the instance. Then `__setattr__` reads `value.rules` \u2192 (rule,). Renders \u2192 `Style(*rules, value=rendered)` where rules returned from `_render_rules`. If `_render_rules` returns the same tuple, `.rules == (rule,)`. Assert `rule in reg.mystyle.rules`. \u2713.\n\nI'm fairly confident. One residual risk: `_render_rules` internals (e.g., maybe it renders nested Styles differently or the default for unknown types isn't \"\"). But since func1 is registered before the first assignment, the only lookup is for a registered type. The only other assumption is that `_render_rules` calls `f(rule)` with the rule as single arg \u2014 implied by `func: Callable` and standard sty behavior. And that it returns `(rendered, rules)` \u2014 confirmed by the unpacking in `__setattr__`.\n\nHmm, what if `_render_rules` calls `f(*rule.args)` instead of `f(rule)`? Then my func taking `(rule)` would fail with too many args... `rule.args` is `[]` (class attr) \u2192 `f()` \u2192 TypeError (missing arg). Hmm. Which is it?\n\nIn real sty, renderfuncs like `rgb_render` are defined as... let me recall. In sty, `set_eightbit_call` sets `self.eightbit_call = renderfuncs.get(rendertype, ...)`. And the actual render functions in sty/renders.py? Hmm, actually sty doesn't have renders.py... Let me think about how fg works: `fg(1, 2, 3)` calls `self.rgb_call(1,2,3)`. And `fg.orange = Style(RgbFg(255, 135, 0), Sgr(1))` \u2014 the Style's str value is the rendered ANSI sequence, computed at assignment time via `_render_rules` with the register's renderfuncs. The renderfuncs for `fg` are set via `fg.set_renderfunc(RgbFg, rgb_render)` etc. in `__init__`? Hmm, actually in sty's `__init__.py`:\n\n```python\nfg = Fg()\nfg.set_renderfunc(RgbFg, lambda r: f\"\\x1b[38;2;{r.r};{r.g};{r.b}m\")\n```\n\nHmm, something like that. Actually I recall in sty/primitive.py or __init__.py:\n\n```python\ndef _rgb_render(r: RgbFg) -> str:\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm. Let me think about `RgbFg`:\n\n```python\nclass RgbFg(RenderType):\n    def __init__(self, r: int, g: int, b: int):\n        self.args = (r, g, b)\n```\n\nHmm wait, does RenderType store args? The get_info showed `class RenderType: args: list = []`. Subclasses like Sgr set `self.args = code` maybe.\n\nAnd the render func \u2014 in sty's actual code (I recall from reading it):\n\n```python\nfg.set_renderfunc(RgbFg, lambda rgb: f\"\\x1b[38;2;{rgb.r};{rgb.g};{rgb.b}m\")\n```\n\nHmm, or maybe renderfuncs take the rule object. Let me think about `_render_rules`: `f = renderfuncs.get(type(rule), lambda x: \"\")` \u2014 the default `lambda x: \"\"` takes ONE argument. And `rendered += f(rule)` \u2014 called with the rule. So renderfuncs are called with the rule object as single argument. The default lambda signature `lambda x: \"\"` strongly implies single-arg calls. So `f(rule)` \u2014 my funcs taking one param are correct. \n\nAlso `set_eightbit_call`: `self.eightbit_call = renderfuncs.get(rendertype, lambda x: x)` \u2014 again single-arg. Consistent.\n\nOK, I'm confident. But hmm, wait \u2014 actually let me reconsider `rendered += f(rule)` vs `rendered += f(*rule.args)`. If it were `f(*rule.args)`, the default would need to be `lambda *x: \"\"`. The shown default in my memory is `lambda x: \"\"`. I'll go with single-arg. Even if I'm wrong about the internals... the test would error, but I've reasoned as carefully as I can with available info.\n\nActually, hold on \u2014 let me reduce even this risk. What if I make the render funcs accept the rule and I ALSO make the assertions not depend on exact call convention? The core coverage requirement is just executing lines 150-156. The value-change assertions are postcondition checks. If `f(rule)` convention is wrong, the test errors at phase 2 \u2014 bad. \n\nAlternative safer design: make the Style's rules EMPTY? Then `_render_rules` renders \"\" trivially, no func called... but then re-rendering also produces \"\" \u2014 no observable change, and line 156 still executes. But then the postcondition \"re-rendered with new func\" is unverifiable. Hmm.\n\nMiddle ground: keep func-based assertions (they verify real behavior) \u2014 I'm confident enough in `f(rule)`. The `lambda x: \"\"` default in `_render_rules` (which I recall from the actual sty source) confirms single-arg calls.\n\nActually, you know, let me also recall the actual sty source for `_render_rules` one more time. From sty GitHub master (primitive.py):\n\n```python\ndef _render_rules(\n    renderfuncs: Renderfuncs, rules: T.List[StylingRule]\n) -> T.Tuple[str, T.List[StylingRule]]:\n    rendered = \"\"\n    for rule in rules:\n        if isinstance(rule, Style):\n            rendered_rule, _ = _render_rules(renderfuncs, rule.rules)\n            rendered += rendered_rule + rule\n        else:\n            f = renderfuncs.get(type(rule), lambda x: \"\")\n            rendered += f(rule)\n    return rendered, rules\n```\n\nYes \u2014 I'm confident this is verbatim from sty. `f(rule)` single arg. \n\nAnd in sty/__init__.py:\n\n```python\nfg = Fg()\nfg.set_renderfunc(RgbFg, lambda rgb: f\"\\x1b[38;2;{rgb.r};{rgb.g};{rgb.b}m\")\n```\n\nHmm, something like that \u2014 single-arg lambdas taking the rule. Consistent.\n\nAlright. Also, my `_DummyRenderType()` instance \u2014 `RenderType` has no `__init__`, so no args needed. But wait \u2014 does `RenderType` define `__init__` that requires args? get_info showed only `args: list = []`. So `RenderType()` works. And `_DummyRenderType()` inherits. Good.\n\nHmm, one more paranoid thought: `RenderType.args: list = []` \u2014 mutable class attribute shared across instances; my funcs don't touch `.args`. Fine.\n\nNow, module-level docstring + imports + helper class + two helper funcs + two tests. Minimal top-level code. No pytest.main. No fixtures needed \u2014 but the instructions emphasized cleanup via monkeypatch \"if appropriate\". Here, everything is instance-local, so no cleanup needed; fresh `Register()` per test is the isolation mechanism. I could add a brief comment noting that. Comments are fine (they're code, not explanation outside the block).\n\nActually \u2014 wait. Let me reconsider one thing: is `Register` exported such that `from sty.primitive import Register, Style` works? Yes, both are defined in sty/primitive.py.\n\nAlso `from sty.rendertype import RenderType` \u2014 confirmed by get_info (sty/rendertype.py).\n\nLet me finalize. Also consider adding `import pytest` and using `monkeypatch` fixture unnecessarily? No \u2014 keep clean.\n\nOne more improvement: in test 1, also assert that calling `set_renderfunc` with a DIFFERENT rendertype adds a second entry (update semantics \"add or replace\")? E.g., phase 3 could use a second dummy type instead of replacing. But replacing also tests \"replace\". Current design tests replace (update of existing key). Could add a third type to test \"add\". Eh \u2014 keep it focused; the dict-equality assertions already verify content precisely.\n\nHmm, actually let me add a tiny bit: in phase 3, register a second render type too, and assert both entries exist. That exercises `dict.update` adding a new key. Small cost, more thorough:\n\n```python\nclass _AnotherRenderType(RenderType):\n    pass\n```\n\nThen phase 3: `reg.set_renderfunc(_AnotherRenderType, _render_func_two)` \u2014 wait, but then mystyle re-render: rules contain only `rule` (MyRenderType) \u2192 rendered with func1 still \u2192 value stays \"<rendered-one>\". That wouldn't demonstrate re-render change. Hmm.\n\nBetter: keep phase 3 as replacement of MyRenderType's func (value changes \u2014 strong evidence of lines 153-156 effect). The \"add new key\" aspect: I can add it in phase 1.5 or just also call `reg.set_renderfunc(_AnotherRenderType, _render_func_one)` in phase 1 and assert dict has both. Let me do:\n\nPhase 1:\n```python\nreg.set_renderfunc(_DummyRenderType, _render_func_one)\nreg.set_renderfunc(_AnotherRenderType, _render_func_one)\nassert reg.renderfuncs == {_DummyRenderType: _render_func_one, _AnotherRenderType: _render_func_one}\n```\n\nPhase 3:\n```python\nreg.set_renderfunc(_DummyRenderType, _render_func_two)\nassert reg.renderfuncs == {_DummyRenderType: _render_func_two, _AnotherRenderType: _render_func_one}\n```\n\nThat tests both add and replace. \n\nFinal structure:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Tests covering Register.set_renderfunc in sty/primitive.py (lines 150