\n\nAlso need to cover the `isinstance(val, Style)` False branch \u2014 automatically covered by all non-Style attrs in `dir(self)` (e.g., `renderfuncs`, `is_muted`, dunders). So one test covers both branches.\n\nNow, potential pitfalls:\n\n1. `dir(self)` includes `__class__`, `__delattr__`, etc. `getattr` works. Only Style instances trigger setattr. Fine.\n\n2. When I assign `reg.mystyle = style` \u2014 goes through `__setattr__` \u2192 renders with empty renderfuncs \u2192 sets a NEW Style object (rendered). So `reg.mystyle is style` will be False! The stored attribute is a new Style created by `__setattr__`. So my assertion should not use `is`. Instead check `isinstance(reg.mystyle, Style)` and value/rules.\n\nWait \u2014 but rules: `rendered_style = Style(*rules, value=rendered)` where `rules` comes from `_render_rules` return \u2014 the original rules tuple. So `reg.mystyle.rules` should contain my original rule objects. Good.\n\n3. Creating a Style: `Style(*rules, value='')` \u2014 signature `__new__(cls, *rules: StylingRule, value: str='')`. So `Style(MyRule())` works with default value ''. Or `Style(MyRule(), value=\"prefix\")`.\n\nHmm wait, StylingRule = Union['Style', RenderType]. So rules can be RenderType instances or Styles. My custom RenderType subclass instance qualifies.\n\n4. Custom RenderType subclass: `class MyRenderType(RenderType): pass` \u2014 RenderType has `args: list = []` class attribute. Instantiating `MyRenderType()` \u2014 fine, no `__init__` defined, so `RenderType()` default. Actually wait, does RenderType define `__init__`? The info shows only `args: list = []`. So `MyRenderType()` creates an instance. As a dict key, it must be hashable \u2014 default object hash, fine.\n\nHmm, but actually, in real sty, RenderType subclasses like `Sgr` have `__init__` that sets `self.args`. The base just has `args: list = []`. Fine.\n\n5. The render func: `func: Callable` \u2014 called as `f(rule)`. So `lambda rule: \"<rendered>\"`. \n\n6. Does `set_