o>\"\n    assert rule in reg.mystyle.rules\n    assert reg.plain == \"untouched\"\n\n\ndef test_set_renderfunc_without_style_attributes():\n    reg = Register()\n    reg.set_renderfunc(_DummyRenderType, _render_one)\n\n    assert reg.renderfuncs == {_DummyRenderType: _render_one}\n    assert reg.is_muted is False\n```\n\nWait \u2014 in test 1, phase 2 assigns `reg.mystyle` AFTER registering both funcs. The style's rule is `_DummyRenderType()` \u2192 rendered with `_render_one` \u2192 \"<rendered-one>\". \u2713.\n\nPhase 3 replaces only `_DummyRenderType`'s func \u2192 mystyle re-renders to \"<rendered-two>\". `_OtherRenderType` entry unchanged. \u2713.\n\nHmm \u2014 one thing: `reg.mystyle is not style_before` \u2014 after phase 3's setattr, a new Style object. But careful: could `Style(*rules, value=rendered)` ever return a cached/interned object? `str.__new__` with a value \u2014 Python may intern short strings, but `Style` instances are regular objects; `str.__new__(cls, value)` creates a new instance each time (no interning for subclass instances... actually, hmm, could `str.__new__` return an interned instance for simple ASCII strings? For str subclasses, `str.__new__(cls, value)` always creates a new instance of cls \u2014 interning applies to exact `str` via `sys.intern` or compile-time, not to `str.__new__` with a subclass. Even for exact str, `str.__new__(str, \"abc\")` may return... hmm, actually CPython's `str.__new__` does NOT return interned singletons; it creates a new object (except for empty string? `str.__new__(str, '')`... I believe it creates new objects; the empty string singleton exists but `str.__new__` with '' \u2014 not sure). \n\nRisk: if value is \"\" \u2014 could `str.__new__` return the interned empty string? Even if it did, it'd be an exact `str`, not `Style`... actually `str.__new__(cls, value)` with cls=Style creates a Style instance; CPython's `unicode_new` handles subclasses by creating a new subclass object. For value='' with a subc