he tuple of rules passed. Good.\n\nNow \u2014 `dir(self)` and `getattr`: in phase 3's loop, `getattr(self, 'mystyle')` returns the current rendered Style. Then `setattr(self, 'mystyle', val)` \u2192 `__setattr__` \u2192 re-render with updated renderfuncs (func2 now). \n\nOne more potential gotcha: `Register.__setattr__` signature is `(self, name, value: Style)` \u2014 type hint says Style but handles non-Style via else branch. Fine.\n\nAnother gotcha: during `__init__`, `self.renderfuncs = {}` goes through `__setattr__` \u2192 not a Style \u2192 `super().__setattr__`. But wait \u2014 `self.is_muted = False` is set AFTER renderfuncs... and `__setattr__` checks `self.is_muted` only in the Style branch. Since renderfuncs/is_muted/etc. aren't Styles, no issue with ordering. Good.\n\nNow, is there any chance that `Register` has class-level Style attributes? The get_info showed the class body with only methods. The \"...\" indicates omitted code though \u2014 there might be more (like `as_dict`, `copy` implementations). But those are methods, not Style attrs. Fine.\n\nNow let me also think: do I need `monkeypatch` at all? Since `renderfuncs` is instance-level and I create a fresh `Register()`, there's no shared state to pollute. I don't touch `sty.fg`/`bg`/`ef`/`rs` module singletons. The custom RenderType subclass is defined at module level in the test file \u2014 that's fine, it's just a new class, no pollution of sty's state.\n\nHmm \u2014 but wait, one thing: could `set_renderfunc`'s loop with `dir(self)` hit any attribute whose getattr has side effects? For a bare Register instance: attrs are `__class__`, `__delattr__`, `__dict__`, `__dir__`, `__doc__`, `__eq__`, ..., `__setattr__`, `__sizeof__`, `__str__`, `__subclasshook__`, `__weakref__`, `as_dict`, `as_namedtuple`, `copy`, `eightbit_call`, `is_muted`, `mute`, `renderfuncs`, `rgb_call`, `set_eightbit_call`, `set_renderfunc`, `set_rgb_call`, `unmute`, plus my `mystyle` and `plain`. All getattr-safe. Good.\n\n`__weakref__` \u2014 getattr works (None or weakref). Not a Style. Fine.\n\nNow, about covering line 156 specifically: `setattr(self, attr_name, val)` \u2014 covered in phase 3 when mystyle is a Style.\n\nAlso the `if isinstance(val, Style):` line 155 \u2014 covered both branches across the loop iterations.\n\nLet me also consider adding a muted-register test? Not needed for 150-156. Keep focused but maybe add a second test for robustness? The instructions: \"Create new pytest test functions that execute all missing lines and branches\". Lines 150-156 include the loop and the if. One test suffices, but I could add a second test verifying replacement semantics / that multiple Style attrs all get re-rendered. Let me add a second test with two Style attributes to strengthen branch coverage of the loop (multiple true iterations). Actually, that's the same lines. Hmm.\n\nMaybe a second test: verify that `set_renderfunc` works when the register has NO Style attributes (loop completes without executing if-body) \u2014 that's already covered in phase 1 of test 1. \n\nI think one comprehensive test is enough, but let me write two tests for clarity:\n\nTest 1: `test_set_renderfunc_registers_and_rerenders_styles` \u2014 the full flow.\n\nTest 2: `test_set_renderfunc_without_style_attributes` \u2014 register with no Style attrs; assert renderfuncs updated and no error. This covers the loop-without-Style branch explicitly.\n\nActually, maybe also verify that a Style assigned BEFORE any renderfunc registration (empty renderfuncs) doesn't blow up \u2014 that relies on the `lambda x: \"\"` default which I'm not 100% sure about. Skip that; keep tests to what I'm confident about.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, let me reconsider the confidence on `_render_rules`. The `Register.__setattr__` code I got:\n\n```python\ndef __setattr__(self, name: str, value: Style):\n    if isinstance(value, Style):\n        if self.is_muted:\n            rendered_style = Style(*value.rules, value='')\n        else:\n            rendered, rules = _render_rules(self.renderfuncs, value.rules)\n            rendered_style = Style(*rules, value=rendered)\n        return super().__setattr__(name, rendered_style)\n    else:\n        return super().__setattr__(name, value)\n```\n\nThis confirms `_render_rules(renderfuncs, rules) -> (rendered, rules)`. The internal behavior for unknown rule types is the only uncertainty. My test design registers func1 BEFORE assigning the style, so the first render uses a known func \u2014 no reliance on unknown-type default. \n\nFor the second element: `rules` returned \u2014 I'll assert `rule in reg.mystyle.rules`. If `_render_rules` returns the same rules object (very likely \u2014 it just collects), this holds. Actually hmm, what if it returns a list and the Style stores... `Style(*rules)` \u2192 `__new__` receives them as `rules` varargs tuple \u2192 `.rules` is a tuple containing the rule. So `rule in reg.mystyle.rules` \u2192 True. Safe.\n\nNow the render funcs: they're called as `f(rule)` \u2014 single argument. So `def func1(rule): return \"[F1]\"`. \n\nLet me now also double-check: `set_renderfunc` param is `rendertype: Type[RenderType]` \u2014 I pass the CLASS `MyRenderType`. And lookup is `type(rule)` = `MyRenderType`. Match. \n\nEdge: `RenderType.args: list = []` \u2014 mutable class attr shared, but I never touch it. Fine.\n\nNow write the test file:\n\n```python\nimport pytest\n\nfrom sty.primitive import Register, Style\nfrom sty.rendertype import RenderType\n\n\nclass _DummyRenderType(RenderType):\n    \"\"\"Custom render type used to test renderfunc registration.\"\"\"\n\n\ndef _render_func_one(rule):\n    return \"<rendered-one>\"\n\n\ndef _render_func_two(rule):\n    return \"<rendered-two>\"\n\n\ndef test_set_renderfunc_registers_func_and_rerenders_style_attributes():\n    reg = Register()\n\n    # --- Phase 1: register a renderfunc while no Style attributes exist yet. ---\n    reg.set_renderfunc(_DummyRenderType, _render_func_one)\n\n    assert reg.renderfuncs == {_DummyRenderType: _render_func_one}\n\n    # --- Phase 2: attach a Style attribute; it is rendered with func one. ---\n    rule = _DummyRenderType()\n    reg.mystyle = Style(rule)\n\n    assert isinstance(reg.mystyle, Style)\n    assert str(reg.mystyle) == \"<rendered-one>\"\n    assert rule in reg.mystyle.rules\n\n    # Non-Style attributes must not be touched by the update loop.\n    reg.plain = \"untouched\"\n    assert reg.plain == \"untouched\"\n\n    # --- Phase 3: replace the renderfunc; the existing Style attribute must\n    # be re-rendered with the new function (lines 153-156). ---\n    reg.set_renderfunc(_DummyRenderType, _render_func_two)\n\n    assert reg.renderfuncs == {_DummyRenderType: _render_func_two}\n    assert isinstance(reg.mystyle, Style)\n    assert str(reg.mystyle) == \"<rendered-two>\"\n    assert rule in reg.mystyle.rules\n    assert reg.plain == \"untouched\"\n\n\ndef test_set_renderfunc_on_register_without_style_attributes():\n    reg = Register()\n    reg.set_renderfunc(_DummyRenderType, _render_func_one)\n\n    assert reg.renderfuncs == {_DummyRenderType: _render_func_one}\n    # No Style attribute existed, so nothing else on the register changed.\n    assert reg.is_muted is False\n```\n\nWait \u2014 in test 2, `reg.set_renderfunc(...)` with no Style attrs: loop runs, no Style found, fine. Covers 150, 153-155 (False branch). Test 1 phase 3 covers 156. Together: full coverage of 150-156.\n\nHmm, but actually \u2014 is test 2 even needed given test 1 phase 1 covers the same? It's redundant but harmless. The instruction says \"Create new pytest test functions that execute all missing lines and branches\" \u2014 plural functions is fine. I'll keep both; test 2 documents the no-style branch. Actually, let me reconsider \u2014 maybe merge into one test to keep it tight? The instructions say \"as little top-level code as possible\" \u2014 two test functions is fine. I'll keep both for clarity of branches.\n\nW