custom funcs... e.g., a rule that's a plain `Style` (nested)? If rule is a Style instance, `_render_rules` does `rendered_rule + rule` \u2014 no lookup needed. Hmm, but nested Style rules could be empty: `Style(Style())`? Wait, `Style()` with no rules and value '' \u2014 then `_render_rules(renderfuncs, (inner_style,))` \u2192 inner is a Style \u2192 recurse with inner.rules = () \u2192 rendered_rule = \"\" \u2192 rendered += \"\" + str(inner) = \"\". So a Style containing a nested empty Style renders to \"\" without any renderfuncs. That avoids relying on the unknown-type default!\n\nBut actually, simpler: I can assign the style with rules that ARE renderable by first registering func1, then... no wait, order issue again.\n\nAlternative: assign the Style attribute BEFORE any set_renderfunc, but with a rule type that's already handled... there are none by default.\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider: maybe I should just trust the `lambda x: \"\"` default. Let me recall the actual sty source more concretely. From sty 0.13.0 primitive.py:\n\n```python\ndef _render_rules(\n    renderfuncs: Renderfuncs, rules: T.List[StylingRule]\n) -> T.Tuple[str, T.List[StylingRule]]:\n    rendered = \"\"\n    for rule in rules:\n        if isinstance(rule, Style):\n            rendered_rule, _ = _render_rules(renderfuncs, rule.rules)\n            rendered += rendered_rule + rule\n        else:\n            f = renderfuncs.get(type(rule), lambda x: \"\")\n            rendered += f(rule)\n    return rendered, rules\n```\n\nYes, I'm quite confident this is the actual implementation \u2014 the `renderfuncs.get(type(rule), lambda x: \"\")` with empty-string default. This is how sty handles unregistered rule types gracefully.\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 actually I recall there might be a check: in `Register.__setattr__`, unmuted path calls `_render_rules`. And in `set_eightbit_call`/`set_rgb_call`... those set `self.eightbit_call = renderfuncs.get(...)`. Not relevant.\n\nOK here's my plan \u20