o` to inspect the relevant symbols: `Register`, `Style`, maybe `sty.fg.FgRegister` etc. The tool `get_info(name)` returns info about a symbol. Let me plan to call:\n\n- `get_info(\"Register\")`\n- `get_info(\"Style\")`\n- maybe `get_info(\"BaseStyle\")`\n- maybe `get_info(\"FgRegister\")` \u2014 but that's in another module; the tool likely resolves names in the current context (sty/primitive.py?). Hmm, the description says \"class, function or method name, as in 'f' for function f or 'C.foo' for method foo in class C.\" So probably it looks up in the module being worked on. Since the code is from sty/primitive.py, `Register` and `Style` should resolve.\n\nI'll call get_info for \"Register\", \"Style\", \"BaseStyle\", and maybe \"Register.mute\".\n\nThen write the test:\n\n```python\nimport sty\nfrom sty import primitive\nfrom sty.primitive import Register, Style\n\n\nclass TestRegisterMute:\n    def test_mute_sets_is_muted_and_rebinds_style_attrs(self, monkeypatch):\n        # Build a fresh register instance to avoid polluting global registers.\n        reg = Register()\n        style_a = Style(30)\n        style_b = Style(40)\n        reg.STYLE_A = style_a\n        reg.NOT_STYLE = \"not-a-style\"\n        reg.STYLE_B = style_b\n\n        assert not getattr(reg, \"is_muted\", False)\n\n        reg.mute()\n\n        assert reg.is_muted is True\n        # Style attributes are re-set (same objects rebound).\n        assert reg.STYLE_A is style_a\n        assert reg.STYLE_B is style_b\n        assert reg.NOT_STYLE == \"not-a-style\"\n```\n\nBut wait \u2014 does `Style(30)` construct fine without registering rendering targets? `Style.__init__` takes `*rules`. `render` needs a registered rendering target, but constructing doesn't. However, `dir(self)` includes many dunder attributes; `getattr` on them is fine. If any attribute is a Style instance, setattr happens.\n\nHmm, but careful: `dir(self)` on a plain `Register()` instance \u2014 attributes include methods like `mute`, plus inherited dunders. None are Style unless we add them. So adding our own Style attributes ensures line 168 executes.\n\nAlternatively, use the real registers from sty, e.g. `sty.fg` \u2014 but mutating those would cause state pollution across tests (setting `is_muted = True` on the global `sty.fg`). We could save/restore with monkeypatch: `monkeypatch.setattr(sty.fg, \"is_muted\", False, raising=False)`? But mute sets attributes on the instance; monkeypatch.setattr on the instance works too and restores afterwards. Actually `monkeypatch.setattr(obj, name, value)` records old value and restores at teardown. But `is_muted` doesn't exist before; `raising=False` allows setting a new attribute, and teardown deletes it? Let me recall: monkeypatch.setattr with raising=False on a non-existent attribute \u2014 during undo, it deletes the attribute if it didn't exist before. Yes, monkeypatch handles that: \"if the attribute did not exist before, it will be removed again\" \u2014 actually I believe monkeypatch.setattr stores the sentinel and on undo deletes if it was absent. Yes, `_setattr` uses `oldval = getattr(target, name, notset)` and on undo, if oldval is notset, `delattr`. Good.\n\nBut simpler and cleaner: create our own Register subclass/instance in the test. That avoids touching globals entirely. Also test that mute works on a subclass with Style attributes \u2014 covering both branch outcomes (Style vs non-Style attributes) since `dir()` yields plenty of non-Style attributes anyway.\n\nHowever, maybe better to also exercise a real register like `sty.ef` to be faithful, using monkeypatch to restore. Hmm. The instruction: \"make VERY SURE to clean up after the test, so as to avoid state pollution; use 'monkeypatch' or 'pytest-mock' if appropriate.\"\n\nApproach: Use a locally-defined subclass of `Register` with Style attributes. No global mutation. That's clean.\n\nBut hold on \u2014 is `Style` exported from `sty.primitive`? Yes, `from sty.primitive import Register, Style` should work. Or `from sty import Style`? `sty/__init__.py` exports `fg, bg, ef, rs, sgr` and maybe `Rule`, `RenderType`... Not sure `Style` is exported at top level. Safer: `from sty.primitive import Register, Style`.\n\nLet me double-check sty's primitive module contents (v1.0.5):\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Primitive types used by sty.\"\"\"\nimport re\nfrom typing import Callable, Iterable, Iterator, List, Optional, Set, Tuple, TypeVar, Union\nfrom weakref import WeakSet\n\n...\n\nclass BaseStyle:\n    _rendering_targets: WeakSet[RenderingTarget] = WeakSet()\n\n    def __init__(self, *rules, rendering_target_name=\"default\"):\n        self.rules = rules\n        self.rendering_target_name = rendering_target_name\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        return self.render()\n\n    def render(self, *values, rendering_target_name=None):\n        rendering_target_name = rendering_target_name or self.rendering_target_name\n        rendering_target = self.get_rendering_target(rendering_target_name)\n        rendered = rendering_target.renderer(*self.rules, *values)\n        return rendered\n\n    @classmethod\n    def register_rendering_target(cls, renderer):\n        cls._rendering_targets.add(RenderingTarget(\"default\", renderer))\n\n    @classmethod\n    def get_rendering_target(cls, rendering_target_name):\n        for target in cls._rendering_targets:\n            if target.name == rendering_target_name:\n                return target\n        raise ValueError(...)\n\n\nclass Style(BaseStyle):\n    def __and__(self, other): ...\n    def __add__(self, other): ...\n    def __radd__(self, other): ...\n    def __call__(self, *values): ...\n    def __iter__(self): ...\n    def __getitem__(self, item): ...\n    def __len__(self): ...\n    def __repr__(self): ...\n    def __eq__...\n```\n\nAnd `Register` as shown. There's also `hexa_to_ints`, `rgb_to_ints`, etc.? Those might be in `sty/lib.py`? Hmm, actually I think `sty/primitive.py` has helper functions `valid`, `valid_rgb`, and classes `RenderingTarget`, `BaseStyle`, `Style`, `Register`. And `sty/lib.py` has `register_auto_style`? Not sure.\n\nAnyway, the key: `Register.mute` iterates `dir(self)` and rebinds Style attributes. Note: `setattr(self, attr_name, val)` \u2014 since `val` is already the attribute value, this effectively does nothing except ensure the attribute exists on the instance (it already does). So the observable effect: `is_muted` becomes True, and Style attributes remain accessible.\n\nA meaningful assertion: after mute, `reg.is_muted is True`; Style attributes still present and identical (`is` check); non-Style attributes unchanged.\n\nEdge: `dir(self)` includes class attributes too; `getattr` retrieves them. For a plain `Register` instance, `dir` gives `['__class__', '__delattr__', ..., 'mute']`. None are Style. So to hit line 168 we must have a Style attribute. Our custom instance with Style attributes hits it.\n\nAlso, to be thorough about \"all missing lines and branches\": the branch at line 167 (`if isinstance(val, Style)`) has two outcomes \u2014 true (line 168) and false (skip). Both occur naturally since most attrs aren't Styles. Good.\n\nShould I also test muting a real register like `sty.ef` with monkeypatch cleanup? Could add a second test that mutes `sty.ef` and then un-mutes/restores via monkeypatch. But `mute` only sets `is_muted=True` and rebinds styles; restoring means deleting `is_muted` and rebinding styles back (they're the same objects anyway). Using monkeypatch on the instance for `is_muted`:\n\n```python\ndef test_mute_on_real_register(monkeypatch):\n    from sty import ef\n    monkeypatch.setattr(ef, \"is_muted\", False, raising=False)\n    ef.mute()\n    assert ef.is_muted is True\n    assert isinstance(ef.BOLD, Style)\n```\n\nOn teardown, monkeypatch deletes `is_muted` from `ef` since it didn't exist before (with raising=False, oldval is the notset sentinel \u2192 delattr on undo). Wait \u2014 does monkeypatch.setattr with raising=False record deletion properly? Looking at pytest source: `MonkeyPatch.setattr(target, name, value, raising=True)`: \n\n```python\noldval = getattr(target, name, notset)\nif raising and oldval is notset:\n    raise AttributeError(...)\n# avoid class descriptors like staticmethod/classmethod\nif inspect.isclass(target):\n    oldval = target.__dict__.get(name, notset)\nself._setattr.append((target, name, oldval))\nsetattr(target, name, value)\n```\n\nAnd `undo`:\n\n```python\nfor obj, name, value in reversed(self._setattr):\n    if value is not notset:\n        setattr(obj, name, value)\n    else:\n        delattr(obj, name)\n```\n\nYes \u2014 deletion handled. \n\nBut hmm, does `ef` (EfRegister) have Style attributes? EfRegister has BOLD, DIM, ITALIC, UNDERL, BLINK, BLINK2, REVERSE, HIDDEN, STRIKE. Yes, they're Style instances. So line 168 executes there too.\n\nActually wait \u2014 is `ef.BOLD` a `Style`? In sty, effects are defined like:\n\n```python\nclass EfRegister(Register):\n    BOLD = Style(1)\n    DIM = Style(2)\n    ...\n```\n\nYes.\n\nBut there's subtlety: `sty.ef` module-level instance \u2014 `ef = EfRegister()`. Its attributes are class-level Styles. `mute` does `setattr(self, attr_name, val)` which creates instance attributes shadowing class ones \u2014 same values though. After monkeypatch undo of `is_muted`, the instance attributes created by `setattr` inside mute persist! Because mute's setattr loop isn't tracked by monkeypatch. That leaves instance attributes on the global `ef` object \u2014 mild state pollution (though semantically harmless since values identical). Hmm. To fully clean, we'd need to delete those instance attrs. That's messy.\n\nAlternative: define our own register instance in the test \u2014 zero pollution. I think that's the cleanest. But maybe also demonstrate with a subclass containing mixed attribute types.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe use `monkeypatch` to replace a global register temporarily? E.g., `monkeypatch.setattr(sty, \"ef\", my_reg)`? Not needed.\n\nSimplest robust test:\n\n```python\nimport pytest\n\nfrom sty.primitive import Register, Style\n\n\nclass _DemoRegister(Register):\n    \"\"\"A minimal register mixing Style and non-Style attributes.\"\"\"\n\n    FIRST = Style(30)\n    SECOND = Style(1, 39)\n    NOT_A_STYLE = \"plain-string\"\n\n\ndef test_mute_sets_flag_and_rebinds_style_attributes():\n    reg = _DemoRegister()\n\n    # Precondition: the register starts out unmuted.\n    assert not hasattr(reg, \"is_muted\")\n\n    reg.mute()\n\n    # Line 163: the muted flag is set.\n    assert reg.is_muted is True\n\n    # Lines 165-168: every Style attribute is visited and re-bound unchanged.\n    assert reg.FIRST is _DemoRegister.FIRST\n    assert reg.SECOND is _DemoRegister.SECOND\n    assert isinstance(reg.FIRST, Style)\n    assert isinstance(reg.SECOND, Style)\n\n    # Non-Style attributes are left untouched (false branch of line 167).\n    assert reg.NOT_A_STYLE == \"plain-string\"\n```\n\nWait \u2014 `assert reg.FIRST is _DemoRegister.FIRST` \u2014 after mute, `reg.FIRST` is an instance attribute set to the same object as the class attribute, so identity holds. Before mute, `reg.FIRST` resolves to class attr. Fine.\n\nHmm, but does `Style(1, 39)` accept multiple rules? `BaseStyle.__init__(self, *rules)` \u2014 yes.\n\nDoes instantiating `Style` require registered rendering targets? No \u2014 registration happens at import of sty modules (e.g., `sty/sgr.py` registers renderers?). Actually `BaseStyle.register_rendering_target` is called somewhere at import time (in `sty/__init__.py` or in each module). Constructing a Style doesn't render, so it's safe regardless.\n\nBut wait \u2014 importing `sty.primitive` alone: does anything register rendering targets? Doesn't matter for this test since we never render.\n\nOne concern: `dir(self)` \u2014 for our instance, includes `FIRST`, `SECOND`, `NOT_A_STYLE`, `mute`, dunders. `getattr` on all fine.\n\nAnother thought: Should the